{
  "comment": [
    "Every visual state of the in-game menu, declared once.",
    "",
    "A state is a set of element overrides, applied through RmlUi's own API by",
    "the offscreen renderer \u2014 the same SetProperty, SetClass and SetPseudoClass",
    "calls the bridge and RmlUi itself make at runtime. It is never an edit to",
    "the markup: matching markup strings would mean this file and the generator",
    "both had to change whenever an element did, which is the duplication",
    "console packages exist to avoid.",
    "",
    "'pseudo' sets a real pseudo-class, so a hover state renders the design's",
    "own :hover rule rather than a second rule written for the screenshot.",
    "A list sets each of its values: a control being captured has both",
    "'focused' and 'capturing', as in the player.",
    "",
    "A state names elements by id, or by position \u2014 'controls-device-list>1' is the second option in the picker, whatever that console calls it, so one declaration renders on every console. scripts/menu_states.py refuses",
    "a state whose elements are not in the document, and refuses two states that",
    "render the same picture \u2014 declaring a state the design does not actually",
    "draw differently is how three of these were the same image.",
    "",
    "A state names the screen it is on ('screen'), which the offscreen renderer",
    "shows as the player shows it: its panel alone, its heading and its footer",
    "(rml-preview --screen). 'set' holds only what the state changes on it.",
    "",
    "Adding a screen means adding entries here. Nothing else changes."
  ],
  "states": {
    "pause-menu": {
